proposal.text:
{
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "During repeated placements, a completed lift sometimes left the held cube already closely aligned over the target at transport height. The observed milestone correctly became cube_lifted and the placement count remained unchanged. The policy nevertheless commanded an additional closed-gripper hover with an upward offset, whereas the recorded next action descended to target-center x,y at z=0.005 m and opened. This is a SUFFICIENCY failure caused by the unconditional separate-alignment requirement, not missing historical information. Replace that requirement with a geometry-dependent rule: at cube_lifted, independently localize the target center and check the current TCP alignment. When lateral center error is approximately 1.5 mm or less and TCP height is within 2 mm of nominal z=0.15 m, descend directly to the localized target center at z=0.005 m and open. Otherwise perform the separate closed-gripper target alignment at nominal z=0.15 m, then descend after observing its completion. Do not use the broader ordinary tracking-error tolerance to decide whether to omit alignment: other repeated lifts still require small corrective hover motions. Apply this direct-descent decision before the minimum-displacement safeguard; do not manufacture an upward hover merely to make an unnecessary alignment executable. Retain the safeguard for necessary near-identical waypoints. Direct descent does not change the observed lift milestone to target_hover: memory remains cube_lifted until release is observed, at which point the existing release and count-update rules already apply. Remove the statements that alignment is always mandatory at cube_lifted and that descent requires a separately observed target_hover in every case."
}

